    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8/e0c89ba4-1407-406d-af50-dc665f216277/16",
       "24x24": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8/e0c89ba4-1407-406d-af50-dc665f216277/24",
       "32x32": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8/e0c89ba4-1407-406d-af50-dc665f216277/32",
       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8/e0c89ba4-1407-406d-af50-dc665f216277/48"
      },
      "displayName": "Dan Field",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a594bb0b0500707c78d8",
      "timeZone": "Europe/London"
     },
     "body": "<p>We could perhaps add an additional CLI argument to sit alongside  the current pidlist opt-in <tt>-p</tt> argument, This argument would need to be mutually exclusive to <tt>-p</tt> in order to make any sense though. I can not think of a use case for requirng both. </p>\n\n\n<p>What I am less clear on, is how the wildcard functionality would work. Was there a specific use-case for this? Presumably it’s usefullness is dependant on the convention of a users PIDs. wildcards for UUIDs don’t make much sense but could perhaps be os use in sequential numeric PIDs or alpha-numeric PIDs. </p>",
     "created": "24/Jan/23 1:04 PM",
     "id": "22787",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/13724/comment/22787",
